A locked BIOS on your Wizbox NUC i9 restricts your ability to change advanced settings (like allocating system RAM to the integrated graphics), but it will not stop you from running a 24/7 local AI agent.Here is exactly how a locked BIOS impacts your two options, and how to get around it without modifying any firmware settings.1. The Software Path (Pure CPU) is Completely UnaffectedYou do not need to access the BIOS to run local AI models on your CPU.Why it still works: Software like Ollama or LM Studio runs entirely within Windows or Linux. It asks your operating system for CPU cycles and standard RAM, which are managed automatically.The Catch: Since your BIOS is locked, you cannot use the "AMD style" trick of manually forcing 16GB or 32GB of RAM to look like dedicated VRAM for the Intel graphics chip.The Solution: Simply tell your AI software to use 100% CPU execution (0% GPU acceleration). Your i9 processor is powerful enough to handle 8B models entirely on its own.2. The eGPU Path (Thunderbolt) usually works out of the boxIf you decide to plug in an external graphics card enclosure (eGPU) to gain true GPU horsepower, a locked BIOS is rarely an obstacle.Why it still works: Thunderbolt/USB4 external graphics support is typically enabled by default on premium Intel i9 NUC motherboards.How to deploy it: When you plug in an eGPU, Windows handles the connection through its standard Intel Thunderbolt drivers. Once the system boots, you just install the regular NVIDIA drivers, and your AI software will immediately detect the new VRAM.The Risk: In very rare corporate IT deployments, security teams block external PCIe devices in the BIOS to prevent data theft. If this is a personal machine, it will almost certainly work automatically.
